= Skin (Dermatology & Venereology)
== Fungal Infections
- *Tineasis / Dermatophytes:* Know systemic names like Candidiasis and Pityriasis versicolor[cite: 76]. Identify the diseases from pictograms and discuss the difference between psoriasis and tinea[cite: 118, 361].
- *Investigations:* Know why 10% KOH is used and what is observed under the dermatophyte microscope (or in candida)[cite: 77, 361]. Know the specific culture media used and the days required for growth[cite: 78].
- *Pharmacology:* Name systemic and topical anti-fungal drugs[cite: 361].

== Viral Infections
- *Herpes Zoster:* Describe the characteristic lesion and be prepared to identify it from a pictogram[cite: 78, 79, 247]. Explain that it is caused by the Varicella-Zoster Virus (VZV) and know what else VZV causes[cite: 79]. Strictly state that the lesion does not cross the midline[cite: 80]. Know the complications and treatment options[cite: 79, 247].

== Bacterial & Parasitic Infections
- *Leprosy:* Know the causative organism, types of leprosy, and its cardinal features[cite: 307]. Differentiate between tuberculoid and lepromatous leprosy[cite: 307]. Identify which nerves are commonly involved and the relevant stains (e.g., Z-N stain) compared to TB stains[cite: 307, 308].
- *Syphilis & STDs:* Provide differentials for a genital ulcer[cite: 125, 279]. Explain the phases of Syphilis (primary, secondary, etc.) with cardinal lesions like gumma and condylomata lata[cite: 125, 279]. Interpret investigations such as VDRL, TPHA, and Dark field microscopy, including how long it takes for VDRL to become negative[cite: 126, 127]. Mention features and complications of Gonorrhea[cite: 287].
- *Scabies:* Interpret a pictogram of infected scabies, identify the lesions, sites, clinical features, and management[cite: 121, 240]. Explain why itching in scabies is worse at night and mention its complications[cite: 122].

== Inflammatory & Immune-Mediated Skin Conditions
- *Psoriasis:* Identify plaque psoriasis from a pictogram[cite: 208, 251, 254]. Know its causes, complications, common joint involvement (psoriatic arthropathy), and specific drugs used[cite: 208, 251, 254, 353, 361]. Note that psoriasis is typically not severely itchy, but if a patient presents with severe itching, be prepared to discuss it[cite: 352]. 
- *Eczema:* Define eczema, list types (atopic vs. irritant), and mention investigations and causes (hypersensitivity)[cite: 119]. 
- *Urticaria & Pruritus:* Know the clinical features of urticaria, its treatment, and state which type of hypersensitivity it represents (Type 1)[cite: 81, 124, 257]. Discuss causes of generalized itching (pruritus), such as in Obstructive Jaundice or Chronic Liver Disease, and the role of antihistamines[cite: 123, 283].

== General Dermatology Findings
- Define and identify primary and secondary skin lesions (ulcers, erosions, bullae, wheals, papules, and vesicles)[cite: 120, 361].
- Describe the "raindrop pattern" seen in chronic arsenicosis[cite: 124, 331].
- Know conditions that cause skin hypopigmentation[cite: 306].
- Describe abnormal nail findings like clubbing, koilonychia, leukonychia, and Mees lines, and know where they are found[cite: 330, 331].

= Paediatrics Viva Board
== Neonatology & Congenital Conditions
- *Prematurity & Mortality:* List complications for a premature neonate (e.g., respiratory, cardiovascular, ocular, hypothermia)[cite: 188, 256]. Discuss "skin-to-skin contact", when sucking/swallowing develops, and surfactant origin[cite: 256]. Know the common causes of neonatal and perinatal mortality in Bangladesh[cite: 92, 201, 211, 359].
- *Perinatal Asphyxia:* Know the definition, how to evaluate it (spontaneous cry, APGAR score), and steps for resuscitation[cite: 201, 211, 342, 359]. Discuss complications like Hypoxic Ischemic Encephalopathy (HIE) and its treatment (e.g., phenobarbital)[cite: 201].
- *Neonatal Jaundice:* Understand physiological vs. pathological jaundice (e.g., appearing in palms/soles) and discuss hemolytic disease of the newborn[cite: 187, 295, 296].
- *Congenital Diseases:* Identify Down Syndrome, Turner Syndrome, and Klinefelter Syndrome, including karyotyping[cite: 91, 196, 314, 315]. Identify Congenital Hypothyroidism from pictograms, discuss clinical features, T3/T4/TSH levels, treatment, and consequences of missing treatment (mental retardation)[cite: 88, 258, 280, 291].

== Nutrition & Fluid Management
- *Malnutrition (SAM):* Interpret Shakir tape parameters and define the criteria for Severe Acute Malnutrition[cite: 186, 244, 344]. Identify the "baggy appearance" sign[cite: 186, 200, 244]. Explain skin changes, complications, and cautiously feeding (F75 and F100)[cite: 186, 228, 244]. Differentiate Kwashiorkor from Marasmus[cite: 94].
- *IYCF:* Define Infant and Young Child Feeding (IYCF), its core components, and breastfeeding attachment/position[cite: 93, 195, 238].
- *Dehydration:* Understand severe acute dehydration treatments and differentiate protocols for babies vs. older children[cite: 94]. Identify IV Cholera saline, its uses, and explain severe dehydration dosage calculations[cite: 95, 184, 259]. Describe the use of Resomal vs. standard ORS[cite: 185, 228].
- *Vitamins & Minerals:* Correlate X-ray findings for Rickets (cupping, fraying, rickety rosary) and Scurvy[cite: 87, 189, 239]. Know treatments like sunlight timing for Rickets[cite: 189].

== Infectious Diseases & Vaccines
- *Measles:* Describe the 3 clinical phases, Koplik's spot (timing and location), brawny desquamation, and SSPE[cite: 181, 244, 280, 292, 340]. Know the IMCI measles classification, Vitamin A dosage/schedule, complications (pneumonia, diarrhea), case definition, and herd immunity with MR vaccines [cite: 98, 181, 244, 280, 291-293, 341].
- *Tuberculosis (TB):* Diagnose from Miliary mottling X-rays, explain investigations (CBC showing lymphocytosis, high ESR, Mantoux test, GeneXpert), and detail treatments[cite: 280, 358, 366]. Explain the role of an NG tube to collect early morning gastric juice for TB testing in children[cite: 280, 366].
- *Kala-azar:* Know the organism (LD bodies, amastigote vs. promastigote), vector (sandfly), and complications (PKDL)[cite: 258, 296, 297, 298, 299].
- *Other Infections:* Discuss organisms causing pneumonia, bronchiolitis vs pneumonia, Guillain-Barré Syndrome (ascending paralysis, molecular mimicry), and D/D of purpuric rash/ITP in dengue season [cite: 95, 96, 182, 217-218, 258, 316, 317].
- *Vaccines:* Know components of the Penta-valent vaccine, BCG vaccine scars, and general vaccination schedules[cite: 97, 98].

== Hematology, Cardio & Instruments
- *Hematology:* Identify Thalassemia from a pictogram, interpret findings (hair-on-end), discuss investigations, treatment (iron chelation), and complications [cite: 60, 249-250, 259]. Understand Hemophilia (factor IX) and its investigations[cite: 60, 196, 200].
- *Cardiology:* Identify Cyanotic spells, Tetralogy of Fallot (TOF), squatting features, murmurs (ASD, VSD, PDA, TOF), and relevant X-ray findings[cite: 97, 191, 248, 312, 365].
- *Instruments:* Identify and explain the uses of a tongue depressor, lumbar puncture needle, bone marrow aspiration needle, Ambu bag, NG Tube, and asthma spacers/inhalers (blue vs. purple) [cite: 89-90, 180, 183, 190, 228, 318, 342-343].

= Paediatrics Viva Short Case
== Anthropometry & Growth
- Measure height/length and weight[cite: 197, 203, 228, 248].
- Plot the measurements accurately on a standard growth chart[cite: 203, 235, 257, 270, 324, 379].
- Interpret growth chart findings (e.g., wasting, stunting, short stature)[cite: 203, 299, 324, 380].
- Identify causes of short stature, specially expecting "familial" as a common cause[cite: 204, 236, 299, 324, 380].
- Measure Occipitofrontal Circumference (OFC)[cite: 203].
- Measure Mid-Upper Arm Circumference (MUAC) using Shakir's tape, including finding the mid-point of the arm and interpreting the findings[cite: 197, 257, 270, 324, 379].

== Abdominal Examination
- Palpate the abdomen for organomegaly (hepatomegaly, splenomegaly)[cite: 99, 197, 203, 235, 243, 253, 257, 270, 299, 325, 380].
- Differentiate between a palpated kidney and an enlarged spleen[cite: 203, 253, 380]. Note that examiners may prefer palpating the kidney from the flank[cite: 204].
- Identify causes of splenomegaly and non-tender hepatomegaly[cite: 100, 236, 243, 248, 269].
- Check for ascites, jaundice, and mild anemia[cite: 99, 236, 325]. Ensure proper measurement of an enlarged spleen using a tape[cite: 380].

== General & Neurological Examination
- Perform a lower limb motor examination to identify Upper Motor Neuron Lesion (UMNL) vs. Lower Motor Neuron Lesion (LMNL), using the plantar reflex[cite: 193].
- List features and causes of UMNL[cite: 194].
- Be prepared for the examiner to use "two toys" to test child development and reflexes[cite: 101].
- Conduct a general examination, check for clubbing (causes and stages), and suggest bedside investigations[cite: 100, 194].

= Short Cases (Medicine)
== Clinical Protocol
- Maintain a strict history sequence: Particulars -> Chief Complaints -> HPI -> Past Illness -> Personal/Family/Treatment/Drug History -> General Exam -> Systemic Exam[cite: 2].
- Use the structural case file order: Salient Features -> Provisional Diagnosis -> Differential Diagnosis[cite: 3, 4].
- *Comorbidity Rule:* Active, relevant comorbidities (e.g., DM, HTN) must not be dumped into "Past History" if they contribute to the present illness[cite: 5].

== Neurological Exams
- *Facial Nerve:* Examine the face, demonstrate a Right-sided Upper Motor Neuron (UMN) facial nerve palsy, and distinguish it from an LMN lesion[cite: 11, 269, 319]. Explain Bell's phenomenon and identify causes[cite: 327, 328].
- *Reflexes:* Demonstrate the Knee Jerk, Ankle Jerk, and Biceps Jerk[cite: 12, 197, 202, 234, 257, 299]. State the root values and causes for diminished, absent, or exaggerated jerks[cite: 12, 234, 252, 257, 378]. Test plantar reflex[cite: 299, 319].
- *Sensory:* Examine vibration and joint position senses[cite: 203]. 
- *Cranial Nerves:* Perform a bedside examination of Cranial Nerve II (Optic Nerve)[cite: 13]. Explain Saturday night palsy[cite: 378]. Evaluate gait and walking patterns[cite: 226, 227].

== Cardiovascular & Respiratory Exams
- *Precordium:* Auscultate for murmurs (e.g., Aortic Stenosis, Mitral Stenosis), interpret character and radiation, and find extra heart sounds (gallop rhythm)[cite: 172, 197, 243, 252, 320, 377]. Identify causes of a shifted apex beat and left parasternal heave[cite: 14, 172, 252].
- *Respiratory:* Auscultate the chest and back to find vesicular breathing with prolonged expiration or basal crepitations[cite: 202, 257]. Percuss the chest (e.g., 5th intercostal space dullness due to liver border) and explain percussion dullness causes[cite: 253, 269].
- *Peripheral Pulses:* Palpate pulses serially[cite: 6]. Check 8 specific points, evaluate the Arteria Dorsalis Pedis (ADP) and Posterior Tibial Artery, and know causes of absent ADP without PVD[cite: 6, 7, 8].

== Abdominal & General Exams
- *Abdomen:* Palpate the abdomen, spleen, and liver[cite: 9, 173, 234, 243, 269]. Assess for shifting dullness, fluid thrill, and identify ascites types[cite: 9, 173, 234]. Apply Courvoisier's Law to distinguish a palpable gallbladder from the liver[cite: 10].
- *Limbs & Extremities:* Assess the lower limb for diabetic foot ulceration, medial digit amputation, sensation loss, and gangrene[cite: 171, 354]. Examine the hands for clubbing, leuconychia, jaundice, tremors, and hepatic facies[cite: 197, 225, 248]. Inspect psoriatic lesions on legs (identifying bandage marks)[cite: 364].
- *Neck:* Inspect the front of the neck, measure JVP, and check for thyroidectomy scar marks[cite: 364, 365].

= Long Cases (Medicine)
== Nephrology & Endocrinology
- *CKD with DM with HTN:* Justify the CKD diagnosis, explain the systemic triad (Anemia, Anorexia, Hypertension), and evaluate for neuropathy (sensory tests, two-point discrimination)[cite: 15, 17, 243, 299]. Explain scars from blood transfusions and outline Renal Replacement Therapy (RRT) indications and anatomical sites[cite: 15, 18]. Be ready to perform Romberg's sign, evaluate vestibular balance, and discuss autonomic neuropathy (e.g., erectile dysfunction, postural hypotension) [cite: 19, 299-301].
- *AKI on CKD in DM with Nephropathy:* Justify the diagnosis, explain diabetic neuropathy (glove and stocking distribution), discuss albuminuria, and explain why scratching/itching occurs[cite: 252].

== Gastroenterology & Hepatology
- *Chronic Liver Disease (CLD):* Present a CLD case with portal hypertension, variceal bleeding, and DM[cite: 363]. Detail the history of how CLD developed, exclude D/Ds, mention stigmata (spider angioma, tremors), check the testicles, evaluate ascites, and discuss hepatic encephalopathy[cite: 234, 363].
- *Obstructive Jaundice (Ca Head of Pancreas):* Justify the diagnosis, rule out Cholangiocarcinoma or periampullary carcinoma, explain the presence of a palpable gallbladder, and discuss tumor markers and investigations [cite: 20, 337-339].
- *Peptic Ulcer Disease (PUD):* Present the case detailing the relationship between pain and eating, weight changes, and required investigations[cite: 175]. Specify which ulcer (gastric vs duodenal) is worse after eating and distinguish the risks of posterior (bleeding) vs. anterior (perforation) ulcers[cite: 179].
- *Acute on Chronic Pancreatitis:* Identify causes like alcohol, drug use, gallstones, Thiazides, or hypercalcemia[cite: 178, 267, 268]. Justify testing for Lipase over Amylase[cite: 177]. Explain how chronic pancreatitis leads to exocrine failure (fat malabsorption, fat-soluble vitamin deficiency) and potential fractures [cite: 272-276]. Discuss imaging (CT, ERCP, MRCP)[cite: 277, 278].

== Cardiology & Pulmonology
- *ALVF with HTN with DM with Peripheral Neuropathy (or Angina):* Present the salient features of Acute Left Ventricular Failure (ALVF)[cite: 248]. Emphasize finding pulmonary basal crepitations, orthopnea, and a gallop rhythm[cite: 371, 372, 376]. Differentially diagnose from Congestive Cardiac Failure (CCF)[cite: 373]. Detail the treatment history (e.g., Furosemide, Spironolactone) and ECG findings (e.g., ST depression for angina)[cite: 374, 375].
- *Pneumonia with DM with HTN:* Identify the causative agents (e.g., Legionella), and relate any observed jaundice or foot ulcers to the complications of diabetes[cite: 256, 257].

== Neurology, Rheumatology & Hematology
- *Stroke:* Present a case with hemiparesis, outline differentials, distinguish between ischemic and hemorrhagic stroke, and detail investigations and treatment[cite: 174].
- *UTI with Psoriatic Arthritis:* Discuss joint movement restriction, back pain involvement (spine involvement), and evaluate using the Straight Leg Raise (SLR) test [cite: 346-350]. Defend the diagnosis against D/D, describe the itchy lesions, and justify prescribed medications (e.g., antihistamines)[cite: 351, 352, 353].
- *Immune Thrombocytopenic Purpura (ITP):* Exclude differentials like leukemia or Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation (DIC) if thrombosis occurs alongside low platelets[cite: 321, 322, 323].